    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The beam pattern matters more than the brand. A compact cross-line is all most homeowners need for shelves, art and backsplashes. A three-plane 360 laser earns its price when you are framing, installing cabinets around a whole kitchen, or setting a drop ceiling. A rotary laser with a detector is the only sensible tool for outdoor grading. Pick the pattern first, then decide whether a green beam is worth the extra money for the light in your rooms.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Laser Levels, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are based on manufacturer specifications, including beam pattern, rated accuracy, visible and detector range, beam color, power source and durability ratings, along with mounting options and recurring themes in owner feedback. We weighted how well each tool fits a realistic set of jobs. No ranking rests on measurements we did not take.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="patterns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Beam Patterns Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>cross-line laser</strong> shows a level line and a plumb line that meet in a cross on the wall in front of it. The lines usually fan out over 110 to 130 degrees, so they cover a single wall or part of two. This is the pattern for hanging pictures, shelves, curtain rods and televisions, setting chair rail and wainscoting, and lining up a backsplash.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>360-degree laser</strong> extends at least the horizontal line all the way around the room. A <strong>three-plane</strong> laser, sometimes called 3x360 or 3D, adds two vertical 360-degree planes at right angles to each other. With one setup in the middle of a room you get a level line on every wall plus two plumb lines crossing the floor and ceiling, which also gives you square. That is what cabinet installers, framers and tile setters use.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dot or plumb lasers</strong> project points instead of lines, typically straight up, straight down and sometimes level in several directions. They transfer a point from floor to ceiling or between walls, which is exactly what you need to center a light fixture over a table or line up recessed lights. Many cross-line lasers add plumb dots, as the Klein and Stabila picks do.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>rotary laser</strong> spins a single beam at high speed to create a plane that can reach hundreds of feet with a detector. It is the outdoor tool for grading, foundations, fences and decks. Indoors it can set a drop ceiling across a large basement, but for small rooms a line laser is quicker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fan angle is the spec that tells you how much wall a line covers. A cross-line with a 120-degree fan set 8 feet from a wall draws a line roughly 27 feet long, which reaches across most rooms. Narrower fans force you to move the tool more often. Three-plane lasers avoid the question entirely, since each plane is a full circle, but they are larger and draw more power to do it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
